{"reference":"2 Kings 25","book":"2 Kings","chapter":25,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"As the conclusion of 2 Kings, this chapter brings the book’s movement from the ministries of Elijah and Elisha, through royal upheaval and Assyrian conquest, to Jerusalem’s destruction and a Judean king living in Babylonian exile.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 25:1-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_025_95b009ace7e2175386acaa27"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Jerusalem Falls, Yet Jehoiachin Lives","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter recounts Babylon’s breach of Jerusalem, the burning of the temple and major buildings, the dismantling of the city, executions, deportations, and the end of Zedekiah’s reign. Its final scene shifts to Jehoiachin: released from prison, honored above other captive kings, and given continual provision at the Babylonian king’s table.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 25:1-21","2 Kings 25:27-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_025_4c3a7926d8f2f7e1e64ee9cc"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"How the Ending Functions","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Jehoiachin’s release does not describe a return to Judah, the rebuilding of the temple, or the restoration of the monarchy. Many readers therefore understand the ending as a restrained note of hope: royal favor and survival continue in exile, but the losses described earlier in the chapter remain unresolved.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 25:27-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_025_8b5a102127eac7f5ac1b9da1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Exile Was Not One Undifferentiated Event","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Administrative tablets from Nebuchadnezzar’s palace record food or oil allocations to a ruler identified as Jehoiachin, king of Judah, along with his dependents and other Judeans. They provide context for his continued recognition and maintenance in Babylon, though they do not confirm the release scene itself. They also help distinguish Jehoiachin’s earlier removal from the later destruction of Jerusalem and further deportations under Zedekiah, so the exile should not be read as a single undifferentiated departure.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 25:1-30","2 Kings 25:27-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_025_ffb20c6cfaf2f6d44d713f08"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}